  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1035 PM CDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\nCloudy skies across the region this evening with a few showers \nover northeast Nebraska. These showers have developed along a\nweak boundary stretching all the way northwest toward Rapid\nCity. These will continue to move north-northeast. The system we\nare watching more closely is a strong upper-level trough over \nColorado. This will bring the showers from western Kansas\nnortheast into eastern Nebraska and western Iowa overnight as\nthe closed low weakens and gets absorbed into a stronger upper-\nlevel wave dropping south out of Alberta/Saskatchewan. Rainfall\nwill continue across our area through Thursday morning ending\nthrough the afternoon as strengthening northerly winds bring in\ndry air.\n\nThursday night into early Friday we are monitoring the potential\nfor strong winds gusting 40 to 50 mph. As the wave moving out of\nCanada phases with the decaying system over the Northern Plains,\nwe see the ridge over the West Coast try and advance. This leads\nto strengthening of the pressure gradient over the Great Plains\nbetween these two systems. A broad swath of strong northerly\nwinds develops over the region including eastern Nebraska and\nwestern Iowa. NBM guidance shows around a 60% chance of winds \ngusting above 45 mph over northeast Nebraska Friday morning.\nCould start as early as 3am, but more likely toward 6AM through\n11am. Winds weaken going into Friday afternoon as the trough \nshifts eastward and the gradient starts to relax, though gusty \nwinds will likely continue until around 6pm. \n\nWith winds becoming calm, clear skies, and a much cooler \nCanadian air mass in place, conditions will be ideal for strong\nradiational cooling overnight Friday into early Saturday. We\nexpect temperatures to fall into the low 30s with a fairly\nwidespread risk for frost. Only spots that might not get cool \nenough for frost may be in our more urban areas of Omaha, \nCouncil Bluffs, and Lincoln. \n\nAs the ridge continues to trek eastward across the central CONUS\nover the weekend, we'll see southwesterly flow move back in\nSaturday trending temperatures warmer over the weekend into \nnext week. Meanwhile, the upper-level pattern remains active. A \nweak disturbance moves through on Monday, though there won't be \nenough moisture for more than an increase in cloud cover through\nthe day. A stronger trough deepens along the West Coast early \nnext week. This system will strengthen southwest flow as it \napproaches midweek bringing temperatures back into the low-to-\nmid 70s for highs on Tuesday and Wednesday. This system brings \nour next chances for showers and storms Wednesday-Friday next \nweek.",
